{
  "id": "PSI-10",
  "summary_plain": "This investigation burns hydrocarbon gases like ethylene and methane around spherical and coflow burners in microgravity to study soot formation and flame extinction. By removing buoyancy-induced flows, researchers can isolate fundamental combustion behaviors that help design cleaner and more efficient flames. The findings improve our fundamental understanding of combustion processes and directly support spacecraft fire safety.",
  "summary_expert": "The Flame Design investigation (ACME) conducted spherical and coflow diffusion flame experiments aboard the International Space Station using ethylene and methane fuels with nitrogen or carbon dioxide diluents. Operating within the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), the project varied reactant dilutions, flow rates, and ambient oxygen mole fractions to examine normal and inverse diffusion flames. The outcomes mapped soot-inception limits, radiative and kinetic extinction boundaries, and flame temperatures, providing quantitative data for evaluating steady-state spherical diffusion flames and refining numerical models.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "Microgravity spherical diffusion flames extinguish when the peak temperature first decreases below 1130 K.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-10",
        "doi": "10.60555/w4zy-bt45",
        "file": "metadata/PSI-10.json#hypothesis",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Soot formation requires a region where the local C/O ratio and temperature exceed critical values, and where the strain rate is sufficiently low.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-10",
        "doi": "10.60555/w4zy-bt45",
        "file": "metadata/PSI-10.json#hypothesis",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "The critical point for radiative extinction was identified for normal and inverse ethylene spherical diffusion flames via atmospheric pressure experiments conducted aboard the International Space Station, combined with a transient numerical model.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-10",
        "doi": "10.60555/w4zy-bt45",
        "file": "documents/PSI-10/PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf",
